A||||3||Fiero Fanatic||Fear Of Timing Gear||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero Fanatic||03-02-2001||12:26 AM||||Any known way to remove the timing gear without removing the cam? Any known way to install without the help of the machine shop?||||reg||1
Z||000001||dman||03-02-2001||04:09 AM||||Hello Fiero Fanatic.<P>Read this thread, <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010116-2-004363.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010116-2-004363.html</A> <P>In particular, read the reply by Phoenix (2nd reply).<P>Also, check out the following article, it details two different methods to fix the timing gear.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/l4-timinggear.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/l4-timinggear.htm</A> <P>The 1st method requires removal of the cam, the 2nd method uses a special tool.<P>It also provides a link to a webpage for this custom tool but that webpage is no longer valid, instead go here if you are interested in that tool. I've never contacted these folks so I don't know if the web page is even valid.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.pontiacfiero.com/ProductsServices/ProdServ.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pontiacfiero.com/ProductsServices/ProdServ.htm</A> <P>Good luck and have fun.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by dman (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||03-02-2001||02:59 PM||||It's easy - if you've got the V6 engine.  Three bolts and the timing gear's off the cam  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  If I can be any less help, just let me know...<P>I should probably shut up - I own one 4-cyl. Fiero, so eventually "my day will come"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.<BR>Good luck with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>   ||||reg||12
Z||000003||862M4inCA||03-02-2001||04:20 PM||||Well, there really isn't any easy way to get to the gear on the duke without lowering the block down enuf to pull the cam, or pulling the engine completely. I pulled mine about 3 months ago and it really wasn't all that difficult with the help of a couple airtools (whoever invented those did NOT get paid enough LOL). I basically pulled most of the stuff off the top of the motor including the intake, alternator and bracket, also a good idea to remove the wiring harness from the engine as it will lower about a foot or two and you don't need more problems.  I raised the rear of my car as high as the floor jack would go and put jackstands down. It also helped with removing the brake caliper and the three nuts at the top of the strut. After that I removed the two bolts at the rear of the car for the cradle and slowly lowered it all down until after some creative work with a tire jack I was able to get the cradle sitting on the ground at the back. with that done it was a piece of cake to pull the cam and have it taken to the machine shop to get the old gear removed and the new one on (I think I was charged $10), and as they say, installation is the reverse of removal.....not a difficult tast, and honestly one that shouldn't be dreaded should you have to do it again. I think it took me 3 days of working on in the middle of winter in a driveway. I would suggest going with an aluminum gear though. I went with the fiber replacement since I HAD to get the gear ASAP and couldn't find anyone who had the aluminum gear in stock.||||reg||1
